WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.016
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4428.

2
00:00:01.016 --> 00:00:02.031
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:02.031 --> 00:00:02.709
<v DOUGLAS MUNGIN>All right.

4
00:00:02.709 --> 00:00:03.386
So, ready?

5
00:00:03.386 --> 00:00:11.512
Today is March twenty-seventh, 2015, and I’m Douglas Mungin representing the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU [Louisiana State University] Libraries.

6
00:00:11.512 --> 00:00:16.929
We are working in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum to document the history of Mossville.

7
00:00:16.929 --> 00:00:20.654
Today we are at Rigmaiden Recreation Center on Old Spanish Trail.

8
00:00:20.654 --> 00:00:24.717
I’m here today with George Hartford Braxton, Junior, and Velma Fay Carheel.

9
00:00:24.717 --> 00:00:26.071
And Velma Fay Carheel.

10
00:00:26.071 --> 00:00:28.441
Thank you for agreeing to be interviewed.

11
00:00:28.441 --> 00:00:31.150
This is going to be a short interview,

12
00:00:31.150 --> 00:00:40.291
about fifteen to twenty minutes and we will likely be in touch with you later to set up a longer interview if you’re interested in continuing on.

13
00:00:40.291 --> 00:00:43.000
Could you please state your full name?

14
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.083
<v GEORGE BRAXTON>George H. Braxton, Junior.

15
00:00:45.083 --> 00:00:46.646
<v VELMA CARHEEL>Velma Fay Carheel.

16
00:00:46.646 --> 00:00:47.167
<v MUNGIN>Perfect.

17
00:00:47.167 --> 00:00:49.771
And when were you born?

18
00:00:49.771 --> 00:00:52.896
When and where were you born?

19
00:00:52.896 --> 00:00:57.062
<v BRAXTON>Born February the eleventh, 1953, in Sulphur, Louisiana.

20
00:00:57.062 --> 00:01:04.354
<v CARHEEL>And I was born February nineteenth, 1957 in Sulphur, Louisiana, at Cal Cam Hospital.

21
00:01:04.354 --> 00:01:08.000
<v MUNGIN>And what are your parents’ names?

22
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:11.000
<v BRAXTON>My father is George H. Braxton.

23
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:14.000
My mother is Mattie Mildred Braxton.

24
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.000
<v CARHEEL>And my mother is Faith [Arie?] Braxton Carheel.

25
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:21.000
And my father is Albert Carheel.

26
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.000
<v MUNGIN>And your grandparents’ names?

27
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:28.000
<v BRAXTON>My grandparents’ names are [Arie?] Viola Towner and [Duff?] Towner.

28
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:36.500
<v CARHEEL>And my grandparents are his parents [laughs] which is Mattie Mildred Towner Braxton and George Hartford Braxton.

29
00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:38.500
<v MUNGIN>And you’re the niece.

30
00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:40.500
<v BRAXTON>But you know something?

31
00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:47.500
We . . . I mean, we only talk about grandparents on one side of the family.

32
00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:49.500
I really wasn’t thinking.

33
00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:53.500
I mean, I got more grandparents than that.

34
00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:55.500
<v MUNGIN>Yeah, with other grandparents?

35
00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:58.000
<v BRAXTON>And she does as well.

36
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:03.000
<v CARHEEL>That’s what I was about to tell him, my . . . [laughs]

37
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:04.000
<v MUNGIN>Yeah, yeah.

38
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:05.000
Sorry.

39
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:14.500
<v BRAXTON>I’m just saying, my grandparents on my father’s side of the family are Henderson Braxton and Annie Mae Braxton.

40
00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:20.000
<v CARHEEL>And on my father’s side it’s Tony Carheel and Frankie Carheel.

41
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:24.000
<v MUNGIN>And what is your earliest memory of Mossville?

42
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:29.000
<v BRAXTON>My earliest memory of Mossville is actually probably an illusion.

43
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:38.500
It was when . . . My earliest memory is when there was a distant relative named Josh Rigmaiden who this Rigmaiden,

44
00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:41.500
some of it’s somewhat named after.

45
00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:50.500
And his funeral was taking place and I recall the funeral procession passing in front of our house.

46
00:02:50.500 --> 00:03:00.500
And to this day, I can swear I seen these little white ghostly figures that was with his funeral [laughs].

47
00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:02.500
That’s my earliest memory.

48
00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:11.500
Well, the funeral was for real, but whether the ghost was real or not, I don't know [laughs].

49
00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:20.500
So that’s my earliest . . . that’s my actual . . . That was easy to answer, because that is my earliest memory.

50
00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:22.500
<v MUNGIN>How old were you?

51
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:24.500
<v BRAXTON>I really don’t know.

52
00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:28.000
I’ve been wanting to figure that out.

53
00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:37.000
I would imagine I was somewhere, four or five years old, maybe, or something along those lines.

54
00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:39.852
<v CARHEEL>My earliest memory . . . Earliest memory.

55
00:03:39.852 --> 00:03:41.563
I don't know.

56
00:03:41.563 --> 00:03:46.697
<v MUNGIN>It doesn’t have to be like the first memory.

57
00:03:46.697 --> 00:03:47.268
<v CARHEEL>[Laughs].

58
00:03:47.268 --> 00:03:47.838
Okay.

59
00:03:47.838 --> 00:03:55.824
My earliest memory, I guess, is hanging out with you guys and just playing.

60
00:03:55.824 --> 00:03:59.246
I remember being sick one time.

61
00:03:59.246 --> 00:04:06.092
Having diphtheria and having to be rushed off to New Orleans Hospital.

62
00:04:06.092 --> 00:04:11.796
I was probably five, six years old, something like that.

63
00:04:11.796 --> 00:04:13.507
But probably five.

64
00:04:13.507 --> 00:04:14.648
<v MUNGIN>What’s diphtheria?

65
00:04:14.648 --> 00:04:15.218
<v CARHEEL>It’s . . .

66
00:04:15.218 --> 00:04:20.923
<v BRAXTON>Diphtheria is a disease that is a very infectious disease.

67
00:04:20.923 --> 00:04:24.915
<v CARHEEL>I was sick for a long time.

68
00:04:24.915 --> 00:04:34.613
<v BRAXTON>In fact we were one of the, the only family I know in my lifetime that was . . .

69
00:04:34.613 --> 00:04:35.183
<v CARHEEL>Quarantined.

70
00:04:35.183 --> 00:04:36.894
<v BRAXTON>We were quarantined.

71
00:04:36.894 --> 00:04:41.458
But it turned out not to be diphtheria.

72
00:04:41.458 --> 00:04:48.873
But until it was . . . I was there, I was a part of it.

73
00:04:48.873 --> 00:04:51.155
But it’s very serious.

74
00:04:51.155 --> 00:04:55.718
It’s something you don’t even hear about today.

75
00:04:55.718 --> 00:04:58.000
It was deadly.

76
00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:04.456
<v CARHEEL>And my memory is different from his, because [laughs] I’ve always been told it was diphtheria.

77
00:05:04.456 --> 00:05:06.474
<v BRAXTON>No, I don't think so.

78
00:05:06.474 --> 00:05:12.930
<v CARHEEL>And I remember after coming back home, for a good while after I come back home,

79
00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:19.386
the nurses would come to the house just to check on me and stuff like that.

80
00:05:19.386 --> 00:05:21.404
<v BRAXTON>Because we were under quarantine.

81
00:05:21.404 --> 00:05:23.825
<v CARHEEL>For a good while after that.

82
00:05:23.825 --> 00:05:26.649
They would just come, just to see.

83
00:05:26.649 --> 00:05:28.667
<v MUNGIN>And so what causes diphtheria?

84
00:05:28.667 --> 00:05:34.316
<v BRAXTON>It’s contagious, just like a . . . You want to think of it like a virus.

85
00:05:34.316 --> 00:05:42.386
Where it was actually, where she caught it from or who she caught it from, I mean, we don’t know.

86
00:05:42.386 --> 00:05:44.000
<v CARHEEL>I don't know.

87
00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:54.492
<v BRAXTON>It may be more environmental in some way, rather than like a virus that spread from person to person, it may be more environmental.

88
00:05:54.492 --> 00:06:03.672
I guess it was . . . okay one of my . . . very close to that early memory that I . . . Was when she was born.

89
00:06:03.672 --> 00:06:11.104
And when she was a tiny baby and they went home, I can remember the sunny day.

90
00:06:11.104 --> 00:06:15.038
Okay, I’m only a couple years older than her.

91
00:06:15.038 --> 00:06:23.781
But I remember that day, and I remember the day I spent on her grandmother’s porch in my short pants.

92
00:06:23.781 --> 00:06:29.464
Because her mother, my sister, is almost like fifteen years older than me.

93
00:06:29.464 --> 00:06:32.087
So she was like my mother.

94
00:06:32.087 --> 00:06:36.459
And that was an exciting day when she went home.

95
00:06:36.459 --> 00:06:42.142
I didn’t know she was going to turn out like this, though [laughs].

96
00:06:42.142 --> 00:06:46.514
<v CARHEEL>He didn’t know I was going to be bossy [laughs].

97
00:06:46.514 --> 00:06:47.388
<v BRAXTON>All right.

98
00:06:47.388 --> 00:06:49.574
<v CARHEEL>But he was so excited.

99
00:06:49.574 --> 00:06:51.760
<v MUNGIN>Just for that one day.

100
00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:52.197
<v BRAXTON>Yeah.

101
00:06:52.197 --> 00:06:55.257
<v CARHEEL>Oh, no it lasted a long time.

102
00:06:55.257 --> 00:06:56.568
He’s still excited.

103
00:06:56.568 --> 00:07:00.503
That’s why he couldn’t come here without me [laughs].

104
00:07:00.503 --> 00:07:04.000
That’s why he drug me here today.

105
00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:07.130
<v MUNGIN>Where did you live growing up?

106
00:07:07.130 --> 00:07:08.696
<v BRAXTON>Mossville I mean . . .

107
00:07:08.696 --> 00:07:09.739
<v MUNGIN>Yeah, where?

108
00:07:09.739 --> 00:07:12.870
<v BRAXTON>More specifically, what do you mean?

109
00:07:12.870 --> 00:07:16.000
<v MUNGIN>Where in Mossville did you live?

110
00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:22.783
<v BRAXTON>From here, well two houses west of Prater Road on Old Spanish Trail.

111
00:07:22.783 --> 00:07:25.913
<v MUNGIN>And could you describe your house?

112
00:07:25.913 --> 00:07:26.435
<v BRAXTON>Yeah.

113
00:07:26.435 --> 00:07:28.522
It was very small.

114
00:07:28.522 --> 00:07:36.348
It had a combined . . . Well, it had a dining room, it had a living area,

115
00:07:36.348 --> 00:07:44.696
two small bedrooms, a very small bathroom and a kitchen, and a front and back porch.

116
00:07:44.696 --> 00:07:47.304
<v CARHEEL>And it still stands today.

117
00:07:47.304 --> 00:07:50.957
<v MUNGIN>And did you also live in Mossville?

118
00:07:50.957 --> 00:07:52.000
<v CARHEEL>[Agrees].

119
00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:52.561
<v MUNGIN>Where?

120
00:07:52.561 --> 00:08:02.098
<v CARHEEL>I lived with him for a while, for a few years, and then I moved several times.

121
00:08:02.098 --> 00:08:05.463
We moved several times around Mossville.

122
00:08:05.463 --> 00:08:12.756
And the last place we lived was in Queensboro, which is down Prater,

123
00:08:12.756 --> 00:08:18.927
just . . . I guess you could say south of where he lived.

124
00:08:18.927 --> 00:08:22.293
<v MUNGIN>And how long ago was that?

125
00:08:22.293 --> 00:08:23.976
<v CARHEEL>I don't know.

126
00:08:23.976 --> 00:08:35.195
That was up until I had . . . No, yeah, right after I had my son, 1978, probably, I moved from there.

127
00:08:35.195 --> 00:08:41.927
<v MUNGIN>And how was Mossville during that time of you guys growing up?

128
00:08:41.927 --> 00:08:45.293
Like what did Mossville look like?

129
00:08:45.293 --> 00:08:46.415
<v CARHEEL>People everywhere.

130
00:08:46.415 --> 00:08:47.537
Families everywhere.

131
00:08:47.537 --> 00:08:51.463
<v BRAXTON>I’m a little bit ahead of her.

132
00:08:51.463 --> 00:08:58.756
And, okay to explain it to you, as far as family was just . . .

133
00:08:58.756 --> 00:08:59.317
<v CARHEEL>Everybody.

134
00:08:59.317 --> 00:09:01.000
<v BRAXTON>Just everybody.

135
00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:01.961
<v CARHEEL>The community.

136
00:09:01.961 --> 00:09:09.646
<v BRAXTON>Okay, my mother’s house from where I was raised at, her sister stayed to the left.

137
00:09:09.646 --> 00:09:12.528
Her brother stayed to the right.

138
00:09:12.528 --> 00:09:16.850
Another brother stayed behind the brother to the right.

139
00:09:16.850 --> 00:09:23.094
Right around the corner, roughly straight line, four hundred yards, was her sister.

140
00:09:23.094 --> 00:09:26.457
Across the street was my mother’s mother.

141
00:09:26.457 --> 00:09:29.819
Behind her, it was my mother’s brother.

142
00:09:29.819 --> 00:09:33.181
And behind her was my mother’s sister.

143
00:09:33.181 --> 00:09:33.661
<v CARHEEL>Him.

144
00:09:33.661 --> 00:09:34.622
<v BRAXTON>And seriously . . .

145
00:09:34.622 --> 00:09:35.102
<v CARHEEL>Yeah.

146
00:09:35.102 --> 00:09:42.787
<v BRAXTON>And then scarcely a half a mile down the road was another brother of my mother.

147
00:09:42.787 --> 00:09:49.031
Now that’s on my father’s . . . I mean my mother’s side of the family.

148
00:09:49.031 --> 00:09:57.197
On my father’s side of the family, the Braxtons, there was . . . I mean it was Braxtons everywhere.

149
00:09:57.197 --> 00:10:00.559
<v CARHEEL>And they lived on what’s called Evergreen.

150
00:10:00.559 --> 00:10:02.000
Evergreen Road.

151
00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:03.403
<v BRAXTON>That’s distant relatives.

152
00:10:03.403 --> 00:10:13.692
But my father’s sister and my father’s . . . One of my father’s brothers, Robert, lived about a half a mile down the street.

153
00:10:13.692 --> 00:10:19.303
And so . . . And that extended family of Braxtons that she spoke of,

154
00:10:19.303 --> 00:10:25.383
in an area we used to call Saprack it was just Braxtons everywhere.

155
00:10:25.383 --> 00:10:36.607
And we all, even though it was distant and [?] we all even to this day have that kinship, sense of kinship between each other.

156
00:10:36.607 --> 00:10:43.622
<v MUNGIN>And so when you guys were kids, you spent a lot of time like outside.

157
00:10:43.622 --> 00:10:46.896
Like could you describe how Mossville looked?

158
00:10:46.896 --> 00:10:52.040
Because it looks different now compared to how it was then.

159
00:10:52.040 --> 00:10:55.781
Could you describe how it looked like then?

160
00:10:55.781 --> 00:10:57.184
<v BRAXTON>Yeah, I can.

161
00:10:57.184 --> 00:11:02.796
I lived on the Old Spanish Trail and as far as traffic,

162
00:11:02.796 --> 00:11:10.746
which is something in the forefront of my thoughts now with all the stuff that’s going on,

163
00:11:10.746 --> 00:11:16.358
it could literally be hours before a vehicle even passed at times.

164
00:11:16.358 --> 00:11:19.164
Especially in the nighttime and such.

165
00:11:19.164 --> 00:11:25.244
And it wasn’t . . . Kids played in the streets, up and down the streets.

166
00:11:25.244 --> 00:11:28.985
People roamed freely from one house to another.

167
00:11:28.985 --> 00:11:33.194
It was like you was raised by the community.

168
00:11:33.194 --> 00:11:36.000
<v CARHEEL>Well, everybody was family, so.

169
00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:41.050
<v BRAXTON>Wednesday was beans day down the street at my friend’s house.

170
00:11:41.050 --> 00:11:48.396
His mother . . . When I found out, I’d just show up and it was a done deal.

171
00:11:48.396 --> 00:11:53.447
I mean it was . . . Everybody was family, clean across the spectrum.

172
00:11:53.447 --> 00:11:58.497
And Mossville ranged several miles long on this Old Spanish Trail.

173
00:11:58.497 --> 00:12:01.252
So I mean, I knew everybody.

174
00:12:01.252 --> 00:12:04.925
And it was . . . I know at one time,

175
00:12:04.925 --> 00:12:12.730
the highest census number ever was seen were . . . Somehow somebody concocted the numbers for the Mossville area.

176
00:12:12.730 --> 00:12:16.862
It was several, I think it was two thousand,

177
00:12:16.862 --> 00:12:25.126
twenty-five hundred people at one point stretched over a several mile area and so it was just community.

178
00:12:25.126 --> 00:12:35.226
And we had a recreation center, we had a schoolhouse, grade one through twelve, which I went ten years to that school.

179
00:12:35.226 --> 00:12:41.195
And that’s why I said I didn’t want to talk about Sulphur earlier.

180
00:12:41.195 --> 00:12:49.000
And we had churches, the church that I was raised in was founded by my great-grandfather.

181
00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:50.850
<v MUNGIN>And what church is that?

182
00:12:50.850 --> 00:12:52.331
<v BRAXTON>Mount Zion Baptist Church.

183
00:12:52.331 --> 00:12:58.622
And he also was the founder of several other churches in the area and in Lake Charles.

184
00:12:58.622 --> 00:13:00.843
And we had a Dairy Queen.

185
00:13:00.843 --> 00:13:03.063
We called it a Dairy Queen.

186
00:13:03.063 --> 00:13:07.874
Right across the street was our Dairy Queen, where they served ice cream.

187
00:13:07.874 --> 00:13:10.094
And just right across from here.

188
00:13:10.094 --> 00:13:12.315
<v CARHEEL>Right across from the recreation center.

189
00:13:12.315 --> 00:13:13.425
<v BRAXTON>We had canteens.

190
00:13:13.425 --> 00:13:17.126
We had a store about a quarter mile this way.

191
00:13:17.126 --> 00:13:20.827
Like today it would be comparable to your Seven Eleven.

192
00:13:20.827 --> 00:13:26.748
We had another one about a quarter mile this way that was a full grocery store,

193
00:13:26.748 --> 00:13:31.559
where you could actually get fresh meat and all that kind of stuff.

194
00:13:31.559 --> 00:13:33.780
But then there was some others.

195
00:13:33.780 --> 00:13:34.890
<v CARHEEL>You had Rougeau’s.

196
00:13:34.890 --> 00:13:35.260
<v BRAXTON>Huh?

197
00:13:35.260 --> 00:13:36.000
<v CARHEEL>Rougeau’s

198
00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:38.789
<v BRAXTON>That’s what I was talking about.

199
00:13:38.789 --> 00:13:41.577
Rougeau’s had fresh meat and stuff.

200
00:13:41.577 --> 00:13:46.225
And it was just everybody was literally a cousin, almost.

201
00:13:46.225 --> 00:13:49.479
I mean, it was literally that way.

202
00:13:49.479 --> 00:13:58.310
It was so many of them, seriously, that you had a great difficulty distinguishing the lineage of how they

203
00:13:58.310 --> 00:13:59.704
<c.p>became your cousin.

204
00:13:59.704 --> 00:14:09.000
So you soon just forgot that and you just knew they was kin and you just left it at that.

205
00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:10.859
That was good enough.

206
00:14:10.859 --> 00:14:14.113
That’s the way it was in Mossville.

207
00:14:14.113 --> 00:14:16.901
<v MUNGIN>Is that the same for you?

208
00:14:16.901 --> 00:14:17.366
<v CARHEEL>Yeah.

209
00:14:17.366 --> 00:14:20.620
Except the lineage I pretty much knew.

210
00:14:20.620 --> 00:14:24.338
Because they would discuss it all the time.

211
00:14:24.338 --> 00:14:31.310
That was a family thing, talking about who came from where and who was who.

212
00:14:31.310 --> 00:14:36.887
<v MUNGIN>Was that a common conversation with people in Mossville, the lineage conversation?

213
00:14:36.887 --> 00:14:38.746
Because from our conversations . . .

214
00:14:38.746 --> 00:14:39.211
<v CARHEEL>Yes.

215
00:14:39.211 --> 00:14:39.676
<v BRAXTON>Extremely.

216
00:14:39.676 --> 00:14:40.141
<v CARHEEL>Yes.

217
00:14:40.141 --> 00:14:41.070
<v BRAXTON>Extremely common.

218
00:14:41.070 --> 00:14:42.000
<v CARHEEL>[Agrees].

219
00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:44.333
<v MUNGIN>And has that always been that way?

220
00:14:44.333 --> 00:14:44.667
<v BRAXTON>Always.

221
00:14:44.667 --> 00:14:46.333
<v CARHEEL>As far as I remember.

222
00:14:46.333 --> 00:14:47.000
<v BRAXTON>Always.

223
00:14:47.000 --> 00:14:48.753
<v MUNGIN>And why is that?

224
00:14:48.753 --> 00:14:53.574
Because I don’t get that from a lot of other communities.

225
00:14:53.574 --> 00:15:04.093
Why do you think that’s one of the core things about Mossville, like one of the things that’s lasted for so long, that conversation?

226
00:15:04.093 --> 00:15:05.846
<v BRAXTON>Well, I don't know.

227
00:15:05.846 --> 00:15:11.981
It was just, this area was, I mean, they had several huge families here.

228
00:15:11.981 --> 00:15:22.500
And I guess maybe somehow those families all married and intermingled and man, you should try to figure that out from a census sheet.

229
00:15:22.500 --> 00:15:26.006
I mean it’s . . . That’s what I’m telling you.

230
00:15:26.006 --> 00:15:29.512
Sooner or later, you just forgot about . . . Cuz?

231
00:15:29.512 --> 00:15:36.086
Good enough because this is very much complicated to figure out exactly how that happened.

232
00:15:36.086 --> 00:15:38.278
You know what I’m saying?

233
00:15:38.278 --> 00:15:42.222
If you want to spend the time doing it.

234
00:15:42.222 --> 00:15:44.852
And so everybody was just kin.

235
00:15:44.852 --> 00:15:47.481
I mean, literally, everybody was kin.

236
00:15:47.481 --> 00:15:49.235
<v CARHEEL>And everybody was close.

237
00:15:49.235 --> 00:15:49.673
<v BRAXTON>Close.

238
00:15:49.673 --> 00:15:51.426
<v CARHEEL>I mean family was . . .

239
00:15:51.426 --> 00:15:53.617
<v BRAXTON>It was a single school . . .

240
00:15:53.617 --> 00:15:55.370
<v CARHEEL>It was just family.

241
00:15:55.370 --> 00:15:58.000
Everybody was family, pretty much.

242
00:15:58.000 --> 00:16:04.282
<v BRAXTON>It was a school grade zero through twelve that was just dedicated to this community.

243
00:16:04.282 --> 00:16:08.051
Okay, and it was all the way until 1970,

244
00:16:08.051 --> 00:16:17.684
it was a totally black school that was devoted to this community and people outside this community did not go to that school.

245
00:16:17.684 --> 00:16:26.897
If you went right to Westlake, for instance, Westlake had a school that serviced a much, much smaller black population than Mossville.

246
00:16:26.897 --> 00:16:32.342
And it’s the same thing when you went to the west, to Sulphur.

247
00:16:32.342 --> 00:16:35.274
I was telling her brother this morning,

248
00:16:35.274 --> 00:16:43.650
I never knew anybody from Sulphur until my last year of school because they never went to school with us.

249
00:16:43.650 --> 00:16:49.094
And they were a much, much smaller group of black people than Mossville.

250
00:16:49.094 --> 00:16:49.932
Much smaller.

251
00:16:49.932 --> 00:16:53.701
I mean, when I say fractional, Westlake and Sulphur.

252
00:16:53.701 --> 00:16:56.632
So it was like this self-contained community.

253
00:16:56.632 --> 00:17:02.915
You had your school, and your school was central to the community at the time.

254
00:17:02.915 --> 00:17:05.427
We had our own recreation center.

255
00:17:05.427 --> 00:17:06.684
It was self-contained.

256
00:17:06.684 --> 00:17:08.778
<v CARHEEL>The school and the churches.

257
00:17:08.778 --> 00:17:11.709
<v BRAXTON>You didn’t have to go nowhere else.

258
00:17:11.709 --> 00:17:17.573
You didn’t go to look and meet nobody else, or go to the churches.

259
00:17:17.573 --> 00:17:21.761
I mean it was all . . . It was a self-contained community.

260
00:17:21.761 --> 00:17:25.111
I don't know if that help you any.

261
00:17:25.111 --> 00:17:25.949
<v MUNGIN>That did.

262
00:17:25.949 --> 00:17:26.786
That did.

263
00:17:26.786 --> 00:17:29.299
<v CARHEEL>And he had millions of stories.

264
00:17:29.299 --> 00:17:32.650
<v BRAXTON>Oh, I was just going to show him.

265
00:17:32.650 --> 00:17:34.325
<v CARHEEL>That’s just a few.

266
00:17:34.325 --> 00:17:36.000
He has stories . . .

267
00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:39.815
<v BRAXTON>See, I have a list on my phone.

268
00:17:39.815 --> 00:17:50.785
This has got nothing to do with this, this is just whenever I would have a thought, that’s why I pulled it out,

269
00:17:50.785 --> 00:17:59.369
because whenever I have a thought about something that happened to me, I jot a note right quick.

270
00:17:59.369 --> 00:18:01.277
Just to flag it.

271
00:18:01.277 --> 00:18:03.185
Something from my childhood.

272
00:18:03.185 --> 00:18:08.908
I’ve been slacking lately, but I’ve got literally hundreds of story ideas.

273
00:18:08.908 --> 00:18:13.677
<v CARHEEL>And that’s what he spent the whole last year doing.

274
00:18:13.677 --> 00:18:16.062
He’s been slacking this year.

275
00:18:16.062 --> 00:18:20.831
But he spent the entire last year doing just that.

276
00:18:20.831 --> 00:18:22.262
<v BRAXTON>I’ve been slacking.

277
00:18:22.262 --> 00:18:27.031
And every once in a while I’d write them up.

278
00:18:27.031 --> 00:18:30.846
<v CARHEEL>Recording stories and sending them to different people.

279
00:18:30.846 --> 00:18:35.615
<v BRAXTON>And I sent them out to the family and such.

280
00:18:35.615 --> 00:18:37.046
But I mean . . .

281
00:18:37.046 --> 00:18:40.385
<v CARHEEL>We’re the elders in this family now.

282
00:18:40.385 --> 00:18:42.292
Everybody before us, gone.

283
00:18:42.292 --> 00:18:46.108
<v MUNGIN>So you guys kind of keep the history?

284
00:18:46.108 --> 00:18:49.446
<v BRAXTON>No. No. That’s just, it’s . . . That’s just . . .

285
00:18:49.446 --> 00:18:50.877
<v CARHEEL>It’s just memories.

286
00:18:50.877 --> 00:18:55.646
<v BRAXTON>In fact all of us . . . And I can tell you,

287
00:18:55.646 --> 00:19:03.754
in the Towner family, side of the family in particular, I’m made up of Braxtons and Towners.

288
00:19:03.754 --> 00:19:09.000
But the Towner side, in particular, very rooted in family.

289
00:19:09.000 --> 00:19:10.872
<v CARHEEL>In family and history.

290
00:19:10.872 --> 00:19:13.213
<v BRAXTON>More so, and family history.

291
00:19:13.213 --> 00:19:20.234
For instance, Butch Lemelle, that’s my first cousin, he was raised next door to me.

292
00:19:20.234 --> 00:19:22.574
That’s my mama’s sister’s son.

293
00:19:22.574 --> 00:19:28.660
And of course all those others here are all kin to me, too.

294
00:19:28.660 --> 00:19:30.532
All of them [laughs].

295
00:19:30.532 --> 00:19:33.809
<v CARHEEL>You figured that out already, huh? [laughs].

296
00:19:33.809 --> 00:19:34.745
<v MUNGIN>First day.

297
00:19:34.745 --> 00:19:35.213
<v BRAXTON>Yeah.

298
00:19:35.213 --> 00:19:38.957
So I forgot where I was at now.

299
00:19:38.957 --> 00:19:40.830
What did I say?

300
00:19:40.830 --> 00:19:42.702
<v CARHEEL>Talking about your stories.

301
00:19:42.702 --> 00:19:45.979
And the last one that you wrote.

302
00:19:45.979 --> 00:19:49.255
<v BRAXTON>Yeah I . . . See, I just jot them.

303
00:19:49.255 --> 00:19:57.681
Every one of these, for instance, every time you see the . . . That was my way of separating them.

304
00:19:57.681 --> 00:20:00.021
Oh, no, that wasn’t it.

305
00:20:00.021 --> 00:20:03.298
But anyway, I got hundreds of them.

306
00:20:03.298 --> 00:20:10.787
I wrote one about why I love my family was the last one I put together.

307
00:20:10.787 --> 00:20:14.064
<v MUNGIN>Could you say that for the interview?

308
00:20:14.064 --> 00:20:15.000
<v CARHEEL>Yes.

309
00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:16.258
<v BRAXTON>What you mean?

310
00:20:16.258 --> 00:20:19.194
<v MUNGIN>Could you read that for the interview?

311
00:20:19.194 --> 00:20:19.613
<v CARHEEL>Yes.

312
00:20:19.613 --> 00:20:21.290
He can read it.

313
00:20:21.290 --> 00:20:22.129
Read it.

314
00:20:22.129 --> 00:20:24.226
<v BRAXTON>Yeah, I guess I can.

315
00:20:24.226 --> 00:20:27.581
It’s kind of . . . It’s a page, I guess.

316
00:20:27.581 --> 00:20:28.839
<v CARHEEL>We have time.

317
00:20:28.839 --> 00:20:30.516
<v BRAXTON>I love my family.

318
00:20:30.516 --> 00:20:33.452
Who is the family that I love?

319
00:20:33.452 --> 00:20:39.742
My parents, all of their descendants, including spouses, and my spouse and children, my sisters,

320
00:20:39.742 --> 00:20:46.452
my brothers, my niece, my nephews, my great-nieces and great-nephews, the spouses of any of those.

321
00:20:46.452 --> 00:20:55.258
I love my family because my mother and older sister, among many others, have taught me to love my family and

322
00:20:55.258 --> 00:20:58.613
<c.p>it feels so good to love my family.

323
00:20:58.613 --> 00:21:00.710
Blood is thicker than water.

324
00:21:00.710 --> 00:21:06.581
When no one would be there for you, family love will never forsake you.

325
00:21:06.581 --> 00:21:15.806
When you die, if no one else mourns you, family will mourn you and miss you, pray for you, think of you.

326
00:21:15.806 --> 00:21:20.839
For their lifetime and love and embrace your descendants for their lifetime.

327
00:21:20.839 --> 00:21:27.129
My family carries a piece of me, and I a piece of them, for always.

328
00:21:27.129 --> 00:21:32.581
Each and every member has created a wonderful memory forever in my mind.

329
00:21:32.581 --> 00:21:37.613
There are not enough words to explain why I love my family.

330
00:21:37.613 --> 00:21:46.000
Look in your heart and you can surely find all the words and reasons that you love your family.

331
00:21:46.000 --> 00:21:56.886
Ever since I was a teenager, I have had a secret dream [laughs] of wanting to live with all my family in a family compound.

332
00:21:56.886 --> 00:21:59.934
So the Braxton-Towner compound, so to speak.

333
00:21:59.934 --> 00:22:07.771
Although my secret dream of all my family living together on the family compound has failed to materialize,

334
00:22:07.771 --> 00:22:13.432
I will never stop dreaming that we always remain a real close family.

335
00:22:13.432 --> 00:22:19.528
Forever, together, all of us talking, laughing, loving, understanding, forgiving, and loving each other.

336
00:22:19.528 --> 00:22:29.978
Not ever dwelling on any bad feelings or feuding from the past, but only looking forward, forgiving and dreaming of a wonderful family future.

337
00:22:29.978 --> 00:22:34.768
I love my family and each and every one of you.

338
00:22:34.768 --> 00:22:35.203
Woo!

339
00:22:35.203 --> 00:22:37.380
This goes on a piece.

340
00:22:37.380 --> 00:22:40.863
I’m the oldest living member of this family.

341
00:22:40.863 --> 00:22:51.314
And when I’m dead and gone, I hope that I leave some memories that can forever be a topic of conversation with the family.

342
00:22:51.314 --> 00:22:53.926
As the oldest in the family,

343
00:22:53.926 --> 00:23:04.376
I also feel the obligation to remind each and every family member that there is no greater love than the love you give to,

344
00:23:04.376 --> 00:23:06.118
and get from, family.

345
00:23:06.118 --> 00:23:13.956
I hope that for each and every member of my family experiences the full extent of that love.

346
00:23:13.956 --> 00:23:25.277
We all need to swallow the negative feelings, stand up, be strong, encourage, and support family love, where feuding and hating has no place at all.

347
00:23:25.277 --> 00:23:27.889
As time passes, nothing is more

348
00:23:27.889 --> 00:23:30.066
<c.p>valuable than love of family.

349
00:23:30.066 --> 00:23:38.775
We should be looking for every opportunity to get together, talk, laugh, love, teach family love to all the family.

350
00:23:38.775 --> 00:23:40.517
Keep family, love strong.

351
00:23:40.517 --> 00:23:41.387
That’s it.

352
00:23:41.387 --> 00:23:42.258
<v MUNGIN>Thank you.

353
00:23:42.258 --> 00:23:42.694
<v BRAXTON>[Pardon]?

354
00:23:42.694 --> 00:23:44.000
<v MUNGIN>Thank you.

355
00:23:44.000 --> 00:23:45.339
<v BRAXTON>You like that?

356
00:23:45.339 --> 00:23:45.785
<v MUNGIN>Yes!

357
00:23:45.785 --> 00:23:50.249
How dare you try to hide that from us! [Laughs].

358
00:23:50.249 --> 00:23:50.695
<v BRAXTON>Okay.

359
00:23:50.695 --> 00:23:51.588
All right.

360
00:23:51.588 --> 00:23:54.266
I didn’t have my reading glasses.

361
00:23:54.266 --> 00:23:56.051
My eyes burning now.

362
00:23:56.051 --> 00:23:57.390
<v CARHEEL>That’s only one.

363
00:23:57.390 --> 00:24:00.960
He writes stories, he’s got lots of stories.

364
00:24:00.960 --> 00:24:05.870
<v MUNGIN>We’re going to ask you for a couple more of those.

365
00:24:05.870 --> 00:24:06.763
All right.

366
00:24:06.763 --> 00:24:11.226
So there’s a couple more questions I want to ask.

367
00:24:11.226 --> 00:24:14.797
So did your family have like a garden?

368
00:24:14.797 --> 00:24:19.706
You were talking about your house and how it was small.

369
00:24:19.706 --> 00:24:23.277
Did you guys have a garden or livestock?

370
00:24:23.277 --> 00:24:26.401
<v BRAXTON>Oh, that was me and my mother.

371
00:24:26.401 --> 00:24:28.633
Yeah, we had a garden.

372
00:24:28.633 --> 00:24:33.096
We . . . Me and my mother in particular worked the garden.

373
00:24:33.096 --> 00:24:37.113
We raised strawberries, green beans, onions, a lot of

374
00:24:37.113 --> 00:24:38.006
<c.p>other [?], peppers.

375
00:24:38.006 --> 00:24:40.684
She was into flowers and stuff.

376
00:24:40.684 --> 00:24:41.576
I wasn’t.

377
00:24:41.576 --> 00:24:47.379
I was . . . If I could eat it, I’d mess with it, you know?

378
00:24:47.379 --> 00:24:49.610
And we raised chickens together.

379
00:24:49.610 --> 00:24:54.520
My mother had kind of a fascination with animals and such.

380
00:24:54.520 --> 00:25:03.000
One time me and her had this plan where we were going to try to sell the eggs.

381
00:25:03.000 --> 00:25:09.186
So one of the things we did way back in the days of black and white TV,

382
00:25:09.186 --> 00:25:15.008
we would sit on the porch at night, which is something people don’t do much anymore.

383
00:25:15.008 --> 00:25:16.464
We would sit outside.

384
00:25:16.464 --> 00:25:19.739
That was a routine thing at night, sit outside.

385
00:25:19.739 --> 00:25:22.286
Even play outside as kids at night.

386
00:25:22.286 --> 00:25:31.019
And we were sitting out one night and we heard this radio station out of Nashville, Tennessee, advertise these chickens for sale, the biddies.

387
00:25:31.019 --> 00:25:33.202
So, it was a great idea.

388
00:25:33.202 --> 00:25:38.296
If we get some biddies, we can raise them, and we’ll have some eggs.

389
00:25:38.296 --> 00:25:40.480
We ordered a hundred of them.

390
00:25:40.480 --> 00:25:44.846
And that was kind of the lot they come in, or whatever.

391
00:25:44.846 --> 00:25:48.121
So we got a hundred . . . One hundred of them.

392
00:25:48.121 --> 00:25:51.032
And we fed them and we raised them.

393
00:25:51.032 --> 00:25:59.038
And as they grow and we’d look and we’d try to figure out, is that a hen or is that a rooster?

394
00:25:59.038 --> 00:26:07.043
Well, to make a long story short, at the end, it was one hundred roosters, not a hen in the bunch! [Laughs].

395
00:26:07.043 --> 00:26:09.954
It was not one hen in the bunch!

396
00:26:09.954 --> 00:26:16.140
And the follow-up on that was one time a cousin of mine was going to go camping.

397
00:26:16.140 --> 00:26:19.415
And I asked my mother to have some wieners.

398
00:26:19.415 --> 00:26:27.784
I said . . . We was kids and we were watching the cowboy shows to understand how to cook on the range, so to speak.

399
00:26:27.784 --> 00:26:29.968
Although we’d never did it before.

400
00:26:29.968 --> 00:26:34.698
So we took these wieners and I’m like, that might not be enough.

401
00:26:34.698 --> 00:26:37.973
So I said, “Mama, what else can I take?”

402
00:26:37.973 --> 00:26:43.795
She say, “Kill one of them damn roosters!” [Laughs] “Take one of them roosters out there.”

403
00:26:43.795 --> 00:26:45.615
So I butchered a rooster.

404
00:26:45.615 --> 00:26:47.798
That’s what we used to do.

405
00:26:47.798 --> 00:26:49.981
We used to butcher our chickens.

406
00:26:49.981 --> 00:26:57.623
Which is something that . . . I don't know what kid know about how to clean . . . They know how to eat a chicken.

407
00:26:57.623 --> 00:26:59.442
But it was a rooster.

408
00:26:59.442 --> 00:27:03.445
So one of those roosters went camping with me one time.

409
00:27:03.445 --> 00:27:04.900
On a stick [laughs].

410
00:27:04.900 --> 00:27:09.631
<v MUNGIN>Velma, did you have any . . . Have a garden or livestock at your house?

411
00:27:09.631 --> 00:27:11.450
<v CARHEEL>Oh, no. Oh, no [laughs].

412
00:27:11.450 --> 00:27:13.997
<v BRAXTON>Velma, you got a lot of memories.

413
00:27:13.997 --> 00:27:18.000
I’m going to have to find some memories for you.

414
00:27:18.000 --> 00:27:20.370
<v CARHEEL>The boys did that stuff.

415
00:27:20.370 --> 00:27:24.162
<v MUNGIN>So what did you guys used to do . . .?

416
00:27:24.162 --> 00:27:28.902
<v CARHEEL>I was very sheltered as a girl at my house.

417
00:27:28.902 --> 00:27:30.798
I had four brothers.

418
00:27:30.798 --> 00:27:32.694
And I was sheltered.

419
00:27:32.694 --> 00:27:36.012
Stuck in the house all the time.

420
00:27:36.012 --> 00:27:39.803
<v MUNGIN>So what did you do in the house?

421
00:27:39.803 --> 00:27:43.121
<v CARHEEL>Cook, clean, can okra, stuff like that.

422
00:27:43.121 --> 00:27:43.595
Pears.

423
00:27:43.595 --> 00:27:45.491
Learned how to cook.

424
00:27:45.491 --> 00:27:48.809
<v MUNGIN>So what did the young kids do?

425
00:27:48.809 --> 00:27:51.179
Where did you guys gather?

426
00:27:51.179 --> 00:27:56.867
Because you were saying like you had, like everyone was a cousin.

427
00:27:56.867 --> 00:28:00.185
So where did you guys hang out?

428
00:28:00.185 --> 00:28:03.977
<v CARHEEL>All over, in each other’s yards . . . All over.

429
00:28:03.977 --> 00:28:11.087
Just . . . Down here, when they got the recreation center together, everybody would come down here.

430
00:28:11.087 --> 00:28:13.931
We’d go to programs at the

431
00:28:13.931 --> 00:28:14.405
<c.p>school.

432
00:28:14.405 --> 00:28:19.145
Everybody would swim right here once we got the pool.

433
00:28:19.145 --> 00:28:20.566
That was fun.

434
00:28:20.566 --> 00:28:24.832
Everybody would come swimming down here and that’s it.

435
00:28:24.832 --> 00:28:28.624
That’s about . . . Just hanging out with family members.

436
00:28:28.624 --> 00:28:39.052
<v BRAXTON>And it used to be, in her time and in my time, the girls in particular used to play jacks, jump rope.

437
00:28:39.052 --> 00:28:40.000
<v CARHEEL>Hopscotch.

438
00:28:40.000 --> 00:28:48.901
<v BRAXTON>They used to play . . . I will never forget her mother, which like again is fifteen years almost older than me.

439
00:28:48.901 --> 00:28:54.686
I can remember when a girl her age . . . I had to be ten,

440
00:28:54.686 --> 00:29:00.471
so even when she was, if she would have been twenty-five or thereabouts,

441
00:29:00.471 --> 00:29:10.262
maybe, it might have been a little bit before that time, because I think it might have been before she was born.

442
00:29:10.262 --> 00:29:18.717
So when she was in her twenties, I remember her and her first cousin her same age playing sticks.

443
00:29:18.717 --> 00:29:27.173
And it’s a game where you’ve got all these colored sticks and you throw them out of a can.

444
00:29:27.173 --> 00:29:35.628
And then you pick them up and try not to disturb the rest of . . . Are you familiar with sticks?

445
00:29:35.628 --> 00:29:36.518
<v UNKNOWN FEMALE>Pickup Sticks.

446
00:29:36.518 --> 00:29:38.298
<v CARHEEL>Pickup Sticks, yeah [laughs].

447
00:29:38.298 --> 00:29:39.634
<v BRAXTON>And playing jacks.

448
00:29:39.634 --> 00:29:48.534
Even . . . And this is even after they was out of high school that this was still . . . It was still prevalent.

449
00:29:48.534 --> 00:29:53.429
Because I wouldn’t even remember it if it was before then.

450
00:29:53.429 --> 00:29:56.099
So and the boys, we hunted.

451
00:29:56.099 --> 00:29:56.990
<v CARHEEL>Shoot marbles.

452
00:29:56.990 --> 00:29:57.880
<v BRAXTON>We fished.

453
00:29:57.880 --> 00:29:59.215
We climbed trees.

454
00:29:59.215 --> 00:30:01.885
We explored the woods around us.

455
00:30:01.885 --> 00:30:05.000
<v CARHEEL>Now I did all that, too.

456
00:30:05.000 --> 00:30:06.200
<v BRAXTON>Yes, you did.

457
00:30:06.200 --> 00:30:08.200
Yeah, she did all that.

458
00:30:08.200 --> 00:30:09.400
<v CARHEEL>I did [laughs].

459
00:30:09.400 --> 00:30:13.400
Because I had all these brothers so . . . And uncles, so.

460
00:30:13.400 --> 00:30:16.200
<v BRAXTON>We, and my mother loved to fish.

461
00:30:16.200 --> 00:30:20.200
So that was extreme family tie in itself for us.

462
00:30:20.200 --> 00:30:21.400
<v CARHEEL>And still is.

463
00:30:21.400 --> 00:30:21.800
<v MUNGIN>Fishing?

464
00:30:21.800 --> 00:30:22.600
<v BRAXTON>Oh, yeah.

465
00:30:22.600 --> 00:30:25.400
<v CARHEEL>Our family, yeah, everybody loves to fish.

466
00:30:25.400 --> 00:30:28.600
<v BRAXTON>We did all kind of things around fishing.

467
00:30:28.600 --> 00:30:29.000
<v CARHEEL>[Agrees].

468
00:30:29.000 --> 00:30:35.400
<v BRAXTON>If you was going on a picnic, it had to be somewhere where you could fish.

469
00:30:35.400 --> 00:30:37.000
<v CARHEEL>Where you could fish.

470
00:30:37.000 --> 00:30:37.800
That’s right.

471
00:30:37.800 --> 00:30:39.800
<v BRAXTON>You know what I’m saying?

472
00:30:39.800 --> 00:30:43.000
<v MUNGIN>And so where would you guys fish?

473
00:30:43.000 --> 00:30:45.839
<v BRAXTON>Oh, there was water everywhere around here.

474
00:30:45.839 --> 00:30:51.111
I mean, we have a place that was frequented off the Houston River.

475
00:30:51.111 --> 00:30:58.411
In the later years, Sam Houston Park, which is actually another branch of Houston River some miles away.

476
00:30:58.411 --> 00:30:59.222
<v CARHEEL>And Ripley’s.

477
00:30:59.222 --> 00:30:59.628
<v BRAXTON>Ponds.

478
00:30:59.628 --> 00:31:01.656
When I was coming up,

479
00:31:01.656 --> 00:31:11.389
it was nearby ponds was one location that has been taken up by the plant now was . . . We used to call it Six Hole.

480
00:31:11.389 --> 00:31:14.228
And we used to go fish there.

481
00:31:14.228 --> 00:31:18.283
I caught my first bass in one of those ponds.

482
00:31:18.283 --> 00:31:26.394
I mean, it was no shortage of places to go fishing at whether it was fresh water or salt water.

483
00:31:26.394 --> 00:31:29.639
One of the unique things about this area.

484
00:31:29.639 --> 00:31:35.317
<v MUNGIN>Kind of following up on that, so, I got like just four questions left.

485
00:31:35.317 --> 00:31:37.344
<v CARHEEL>And we’d go hiking, too.

486
00:31:37.344 --> 00:31:37.750
<v BRAXTON>[Agrees].

487
00:31:37.750 --> 00:31:41.806
<v CARHEEL>Some mornings we’d get up really, really early, before day.

488
00:31:41.806 --> 00:31:47.078
And hike down the railroad track, go all the way to Houston River.

489
00:31:47.078 --> 00:31:49.106
Which was a long [laughs] . . .

490
00:31:49.106 --> 00:31:52.756
<v BRAXTON>Which is the same thing I was referring to.

491
00:31:52.756 --> 00:31:54.378
I called it exploring.

492
00:31:54.378 --> 00:31:54.783
<v CARHEEL>Yes.

493
00:31:54.783 --> 00:31:56.000
Yeah [laughs].

494
00:31:56.000 --> 00:31:58.203
<v BRAXTON>And that’s simply what it was.

495
00:31:58.203 --> 00:32:04.812
You just went and see what you . . . And you would be shocked and surprised at all the interesting . . .

496
00:32:04.812 --> 00:32:06.281
<v CARHEEL>Babies and all, everybody.

497
00:32:06.281 --> 00:32:08.117
<v BRAXTON>Things that you would encounter.

498
00:32:08.117 --> 00:32:08.852
<v CARHEEL>Oh, yeah.

499
00:32:08.852 --> 00:32:09.586
<v BRAXTON>You know?

500
00:32:09.586 --> 00:32:13.258
And that was one of the things you just did.

501
00:32:13.258 --> 00:32:18.398
You just like, I don't know if you called it, I called it exploring.

502
00:32:18.398 --> 00:32:22.438
But we would just say, “Hey, let’s go down the Brimstone.”

503
00:32:22.438 --> 00:32:24.641
And everybody knew what that meant.

504
00:32:24.641 --> 00:32:25.008
<v CARHEEL>[Agrees].

505
00:32:25.008 --> 00:32:30.148
<v BRAXTON>If you went down the Brimstone, you wasn’t really going to do nothing special.

506
00:32:30.148 --> 00:32:34.922
You just going down the Brimstone, and you’d see what you could see.

507
00:32:34.922 --> 00:32:35.289
<v CARHEEL>[Agrees].

508
00:32:35.289 --> 00:32:38.227
And we had lots of like fruit trees.

509
00:32:38.227 --> 00:32:42.266
And in Grandma’s backyard, we had pear trees, pecans, hickory nuts.

510
00:32:42.266 --> 00:32:43.000
<v BRAXTON>Figs.

511
00:32:43.000 --> 00:32:43.414
<v CARHEEL>Figs.

512
00:32:43.414 --> 00:32:45.485
Persimmons in the front yard.

513
00:32:45.485 --> 00:32:49.627
And then in our backyard, we had peaches and pecans.

514
00:32:49.627 --> 00:32:50.041
<v BRAXTON>Pecans.

515
00:32:50.041 --> 00:32:52.112
<v CARHEEL>All kinds of fruit trees.

516
00:32:52.112 --> 00:32:57.083
We spent a lot of time when it’s in season, doing that.

517
00:32:57.083 --> 00:32:58.325
Picking mayhaws, blackberries.

518
00:32:58.325 --> 00:33:04.953
<v BRAXTON>I went yesterday because I thought about the pecan tree I fell out of one time.

519
00:33:04.953 --> 00:33:08.266
I was trying to impress two older girls.

520
00:33:08.266 --> 00:33:13.237
And I hadn’t really seen it in quite some time, that tree.

521
00:33:13.237 --> 00:33:15.722
And I thought it was gone.

522
00:33:15.722 --> 00:33:16.964
But actually yesterday . . .

523
00:33:16.964 --> 00:33:18.207
<v CARHEEL>It’s still there.

524
00:33:18.207 --> 00:33:21.935
<v BRAXTON>I went and I saw the tree still exists.

525
00:33:21.935 --> 00:33:22.349
<v CARHEEL>[Agrees]

526
00:33:22.349 --> 00:33:25.249
<v BRAXTON>Both of the trees are still up.

527
00:33:25.249 --> 00:33:28.562
But they . . . One of them is almost dead.

528
00:33:28.562 --> 00:33:30.633
It’s just barely hanging on.

529
00:33:30.633 --> 00:33:33.533
But that was the neighborhood pecan tree.

530
00:33:33.533 --> 00:33:37.675
And you met a whole lot of people every day . . .

531
00:33:37.675 --> 00:33:40.160
<v CARHEEL>Under that pecan tree, yeah. [laughs]

532
00:33:40.160 --> 00:33:45.544
<v BRAXTON>Like I was trying to impress the older girls that came up there.

533
00:33:45.544 --> 00:33:48.030
And fell out of the tree.

534
00:33:48.030 --> 00:33:53.000
So you had all kind of things that filled your time.

535
00:33:53.000 --> 00:33:56.000
<v CARHEEL>Do you remember that time you all had that hose, water hose?

536
00:33:56.000 --> 00:33:58.750
You all tied it in the top of the tree and . . .

537
00:33:58.750 --> 00:33:59.000
<v BRAXTON>Yeah.

538
00:33:59.000 --> 00:34:01.750
<v CARHEEL>And would put a tire on it and a swing [laughs].

539
00:34:01.750 --> 00:34:02.250
<v BRAXTON>Oh, yeah.

540
00:34:02.250 --> 00:34:02.500
<v CARHEEL>Yeah.

541
00:34:02.500 --> 00:34:04.750
They used to do all that kind of stuff.

542
00:34:04.750 --> 00:34:05.250
<v MUNGIN>All right.

543
00:34:05.250 --> 00:34:06.750
So it’s six o’clock right now.

544
00:34:06.750 --> 00:34:07.500
We’re going to . . .

545
00:34:07.500 --> 00:34:07.750
<v BRAXTON>Whoa!

546
00:34:07.750 --> 00:34:08.000
<v MUNGIN>Yeah.

547
00:34:08.000 --> 00:34:09.500
Let’s get you out of here.

548
00:34:09.500 --> 00:34:14.500
So just three quick questions for you guys and then I’m going to let you guys go for the day.

549
00:34:14.500 --> 00:34:15.000
<v BRAXTON>Sure.

550
00:34:15.000 --> 00:34:17.560
<v MUNGIN>What does home mean to you?

551
00:34:17.560 --> 00:34:23.533
So we keep hearing that word a lot, I’ve asked you that question before.

552
00:34:23.533 --> 00:34:26.520
But what does home mean to you?

553
00:34:26.520 --> 00:34:26.947
<v CARHEEL>Family.

554
00:34:26.947 --> 00:34:30.787
Place to always be able to go back to.

555
00:34:30.787 --> 00:34:35.053
No matter where you go, you can always come home.

556
00:34:35.053 --> 00:34:44.440
Home is that place where it’s going to always be somebody there that you know, somebody waiting, somebody want to see you.

557
00:34:44.440 --> 00:34:49.133
<v BRAXTON>But not only that, it’s a comfortable place to come to.

558
00:34:49.133 --> 00:34:55.533
Home is where you come to find comfort, and where you come to find family.

559
00:34:55.533 --> 00:34:58.520
It’s more than just a physical structure.

560
00:34:58.520 --> 00:35:03.213
It’s got much more than being just a physical place, a [?].

561
00:35:03.213 --> 00:35:03.640
<v CARHEEL>Yeah.

562
00:35:03.640 --> 00:35:04.493
It’s family.

563
00:35:04.493 --> 00:35:07.053
<v MUNGIN>What does community mean to you?

564
00:35:07.053 --> 00:35:09.613
<v BRAXTON>Here it’s the same as home.

565
00:35:09.613 --> 00:35:11.320
<v CARHEEL>Yeah, because it’s family.

566
00:35:11.320 --> 00:35:17.720
<v BRAXTON>That’s what I’ve been trying to . . . Point I’ve been trying to get across to you.

567
00:35:17.720 --> 00:35:19.000
<v CARHEEL>Yeah. [Agrees]

568
00:35:19.000 --> 00:35:22.394
<v BRAXTON>It was . . . It’s the same as home.

569
00:35:22.394 --> 00:35:23.848
<v CARHEEL>Community is family.

570
00:35:23.848 --> 00:35:24.818
<v BRAXTON>It’s inseparable.

571
00:35:24.818 --> 00:35:25.303
<v CARHEEL>[Agrees]

572
00:35:25.303 --> 00:35:32.091
<v BRAXTON>I don't know no other way to tell it to you, but it’s inseparable.

573
00:35:32.091 --> 00:35:32.576
<v CARHEEL>Yeah.

574
00:35:32.576 --> 00:35:38.394
<v MUNGIN>What’s the most important thing you want people to know about Mossville?

575
00:35:38.394 --> 00:35:41.303
It’s not in the history books.

576
00:35:41.303 --> 00:35:45.667
A lot of folks don’t know about this town.

577
00:35:45.667 --> 00:35:48.576
<v BRAXTON>That it was one big family.

578
00:35:48.576 --> 00:35:52.455
We had . . . See, I’ve been different things here.

579
00:35:52.455 --> 00:36:00.697
Some years back, we had . . . We started in the ‘80s, we started to have a family reunion.

580
00:36:00.697 --> 00:36:02.152
And we soon

581
00:36:02.152 --> 00:36:06.030
<c.p>come to realize that you couldn't do that.

582
00:36:06.030 --> 00:36:08.939
Because the whole community is family!

583
00:36:08.939 --> 00:36:19.606
And suddenly it turned into a community reunion, and it was right here in this building is where it took place at.

584
00:36:19.606 --> 00:36:25.424
And we had a parade, right down through the middle of Mossville.

585
00:36:25.424 --> 00:36:31.242
Have you rode the boundaries of Mossville, to know where they’re at?

586
00:36:31.242 --> 00:36:33.667
Well, we had a parade.

587
00:36:33.667 --> 00:36:42.394
And we had . . . The Lake Charles American Press did a big spread on it in the American Press.

588
00:36:42.394 --> 00:36:47.727
And that day, I boiled all the shrimp for the festivities.

589
00:36:47.727 --> 00:36:55.485
And we had . . . People came in from California, and here and there, and blah, blah, blah.

590
00:36:55.485 --> 00:36:57.424
So Mossville is home.

591
00:36:57.424 --> 00:37:08.576
I mean, the house that you lived in may have a little higher . . . Be a little higher on your list of defining home.

592
00:37:08.576 --> 00:37:11.000
<v UNKNOWN FEMALE>They’re kicking us out.

593
00:37:11.000 --> 00:37:12.152
<v MUNGIN>They’re kicking us out?

594
00:37:12.152 --> 00:37:12.727
All right.

595
00:37:12.727 --> 00:37:15.318
So we’re about to get kicked out right now.

596
00:37:15.318 --> 00:37:16.758
So our time is up.

597
00:37:16.758 --> 00:37:18.485
So on behalf of the center . . .

598
00:37:18.485 --> 00:37:19.924
<v BRAXTON>What was your last question?

599
00:37:19.924 --> 00:37:21.364
<v MUNGIN>That was the last question.

600
00:37:21.364 --> 00:37:22.227
<v BRAXTON>Was it really?

601
00:37:22.227 --> 00:37:22.515
Okay.

602
00:37:22.515 --> 00:37:22.803
<v MUNGIN>Yeah.

603
00:37:22.803 --> 00:37:23.667
That was it.

604
00:37:23.667 --> 00:37:27.409
So on behalf of the center and museum, we appreciate your contribution today.

605
00:37:27.409 --> 00:37:28.848
So I think that’s it.

606
00:37:28.848 --> 00:37:30.000
You can press . . .

607
00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:30.429
<c.p>[End Tape 4428.

608
00:37:30.429 --> 00:37:30.857
End Session I.]
